osely when it comes  to that transformation of the industry  when it comes to making that green and  this is of course a medium to long-term  prospect but germany hopes that it can  convince canada to use some of its  untapped potential and also explore  liquid natural gas on the east coast and  then hopefully export that to germany so  and also more importantly perhaps use um  the hydrogen potential in the country  and there is the hope that in three to  six years time perhaps the first  hydrogen can be exported from canada to  germany earlier i spoke to energy  analyst thomas o'donnell about how much  green energy cooperation with canada can  help europe out with its current crisis  canada cannot supply any renewable  energy that will make any difference in  the current confrontation with russia  they're spending some time going to a a  small city  near the northern coast of canada i  believe in new brunswick to look at  where canada  says they will in the future  produce hydrogen green hydrogen now this  is sort of one of those things that's  very important to do when you meet a  german  chancellor and especially mr halbach the  green super minister of environment and  energy um but really what they're after  i think in the immediate is minerals  uh we can talk about that rare earths  and so forth and copper but also lng but  even that canada can't provide it for  some years lng  i wanted to take that up so trudeau was  asked when will canada deliver lng to  germany and how much he said he was  looking for the business case to do so  what does he mean by that exactly  uh that means he's not convinced he  should do it  there's something  i'll admit as the expert here i wrote  some articles on  germany's real lng policy  about four or five years ago in 2018  when the previous coalition decided in  its initial treaty  under merkel that they would fund and  support lng import terminals in northern  germany which were purely for liquid for  transportation but it never happened and  during that time one thing they did is  they said they were going to contribute  a half a million dollars  in canada on the coast for  building a facility that would export  lng  nothing ever happened afterwards  it was it died and here they are again  bringing it up and canada according to  trudeau seemingly isn't yet committed so  it doesn't sound like you think germany  should count on canadian lng but we're  seeing european energy prices spike  again this week after this announcement  of a three-day shutdown of nordstrom one  at the end of the month how do you rate  germany's chances of successfully  replacing shrinking gas supplies quickly  it's simply not possible  and don't take it on my authority uh  listen to mr mueller who's head of the  network agency for the natural gas  infrastructure in germany he says this  will be a crisis of two years if putin  cuts off the big pipelines he said this  publicly he's absolutely right if he  cuts off the big pipelines storage a  won't be filled if he cuts it off now b  if when he cuts it off later storage  isn't enough you can't replace those  huge pipeline imports  simply with storage after two three  months there's a debate now  it would run out and this would be a  quite a crisis and there's no reason to  believe this won't repeat for two years  it'll take that long to find other  supplies  from other countries  thank you very much energy analyst  thomas o'donnell there  you
 

